A||||7||HotFieroGT||Supercharging vs turbocharging||||5
Z||000000||HotFieroGT||10-30-1999||12:39 AM||||I have read a lot on turbocharging in the forum but have not seen much on supercharging.  I am a bit wary of adding a turbo to my car because of the extra heat generated by a turbo.  I already have problems with the plastic parts around the engine becoming brittle and I burn up a coil every couple of years from the heat generated by this mid engine design.  What is the consensus on a supercharger? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Greg Piet||10-30-1999||02:33 AM||||Well my first thought wasa  supercharger as well, but look a the engine and compartment.<BR>there is room for a blower by the tranny, but there are pullys only on the other side of the engine.  So there has to be a funky setup to drive the supercharger.  There is a guy in  New Jersey that did it, but the turbo seems magnatudes easier to implement.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||10-30-1999||07:03 AM||||Here is a custom supercharger setup for the Fiero V6:<BR> <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/eng_per.html" TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/eng_per.html</A> <P><BR>But, having NO air conditioning does not appeal to me.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||jlhuber||10-30-1999||09:52 AM||||no air, shoot my 86.5 gt has air but i never use it anyway...that is what the t-tops are for.  i'm going with a super for i agree with hotfierogt||||reg||14
Z||000004||TOM||10-30-1999||10:51 AM||||Interesting for a centrifigal supercharger. But what is the cost. I can do a turbo addition for about $500. Plus ie is easily changed back to stock||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroshop||10-30-1999||11:08 AM||||Tom what are you using for a turbo. Let me know caus I'd like to build one for myself and possibly for others.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||10-30-1999||11:37 PM||||The Fiero is a mixed bag for that decision. Superchargers require little plumbing vs the turbo's oil and coolant lines. But as some said, there is little room in there. It is possible to have an adapter plate milled for the bottom intake to handle the fuel rails and bolt an Eaton M-62 or M-90 on there, but that's as much work as doing the plumbing.<P>As for power, you can overboost far easier with a turbo (good), but there's nothing like the bottom end torque of a supercharger.<P>I know there was one guy that mounted a Paxton or Vortec back by the trans and ran a drive shaft back to it. That might be an option. Maybe move the battery and mount it backwards in that area driven by the crank.<P>Then there is the ECM code .....||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||10-31-1999||01:22 AM||||Some mustangs with paxton's have them mounted backwards and they work great. For me the plumbing for a turbo is no big deal.||||reg||1
